WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.020
A use-after-free defect occurs when code accesses memory after that memory has been released.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:14.920
The access might be a read, a write, a call that passes the pointer as valid storage, or logic that treats the old address as an active object.

3
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:21.420
The key issue is lifetime: the program is using storage after the object it represented is no longer alive.

4
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.580
The pointer value may still look like an address.

5
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:31.000
It may be non-null, it may compare equal to a saved value, and it may even appear to work in a small test.

6
00:00:31.100 --> 00:00:33.740
None of that proves the object is still valid.

7
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:41.440
Once the allocation is released, the program no longer owns that object, and later use depends on an assumption the program is not allowed to make.

8
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:49.400
Freed memory must not be read, written, passed to APIs as valid storage, or interpreted as an active object.

9
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.300
The memory manager may reuse that storage later.

10
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.160
It may leave it untouched for a while.

11
00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:57.380
It may add diagnostics in a debug build.

12
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.360
Defensive C code should not rely on any of those implementation details.

13
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:12.160
Use-after-free defects can cause crashes, corrupted state, unpredictable behavior, accidental data exposure, or security defects.

14
00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:20.500
The safe engineering response is to prevent stale references from surviving release, and to make ownership and cleanup rules visible enough for review.

15
00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.820
Prevention begins by treating lifetime as a design rule, not as an afterthought.

16
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.760
Every allocation should have an owner.

17
00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.900
Every borrowed pointer should have a clear lifetime.

18
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:37.320
Every cleanup path should leave the program in a state where later code cannot accidentally continue using released storage.
